                {
                  "label": "Pub Title",
                  "value": "This Is the Heart of Crumbling and Doomed Berlin. (Drawn by Howard Burke — February 15, 1945)"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Pub Note",
                  "value": "The map is an illustrated panoramic view of central Berlin, rendered in perspective with a reddish tint, showing the city as seen roughly from the southwest looking northeast. It emphasizes the core government and military district at the end of World War II — a symbolic representation of Nazi Germany’s downfall. Prominent Landmarks Labeled: Reich Chancellery (RICHS CHANCELLERY) — near the map’s center, marked by a small swastika, identified as Hitler’s stronghold. Reich Ministry of Aviation, National Theatre, Stock Exchange, City Hall, and Alexander Platz appear toward the right (east). Brandenburg Gate and Unter den Linden are clearly shown leading eastward from the Tiergarten. Reichstag, War Ministry, and Gestapo Headquarters lie nearby, forming the political core. Tempelhof Airport, Zoological Garden, Army Headquarters, and several military barracks appear along the edges. Charlottenburg, Moabit, and Tiergarten districts are labeled. Style and Intent: The map combines cartographic accuracy with propagandistic tone — showing the German capital as the collapsing “heart” of the Nazi empire. It emphasizes bomb damage, Soviet encirclement, and moral judgment through its dramatic title and textual framing. Visual Tone: The reddish sepia palette and heavy shadows evoke both fire and ruin. The buildings are drawn in a three-dimensional, almost architectural rendering, while key landmarks are enlarged for clarity. Publisher Context: Published by the Milwaukee Sentinel during the final months of World War II, this illustrated map was both news and morale material, showing readers how close the Allies were to Berlin’s fall."
                },
